WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.433
Well-managed credentials can still carry
excessive authority.

2
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:05.780
Give each service identity the operations

3
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.931
and resources its workload requires.

4
00:00:07.931 --> 00:00:11.255
Scope access to the relevant database,
storage container,

5
00:00:11.255 --> 00:00:14.481
queue, topic, secret namespace, and tenant
boundary.

6
00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:17.414
Separate routine application access from
administrative operations.

7
00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:20.543
A worker that reads one queue should not

8
00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:23.867
inherit permission to administer the
entire messaging service.

9
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.094
Use separate identities for workloads with
different responsibilities,

10
00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:30.711
and deliberately separate development,
test, staging, and production.

11
00:00:30.711 --> 00:00:34.035
Developer access should not automatically
confer production authority.

12
00:00:34.035 --> 00:00:37.164
Deployment identities may need permissions
to release software

13
00:00:37.164 --> 00:00:40.097
that the running application does not
need.

14
00:00:40.097 --> 00:00:43.617
Avoid sharing a broad production identity
merely to simplify

15
00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:47.430
configuration; shared authority also makes
audit records less useful.

16
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.656
For continuous integration and deployment,
prefer supported workload

17
00:00:50.656 --> 00:00:54.372
federation over permanent cloud keys
stored as pipeline variables.

18
00:00:54.372 --> 00:00:58.185
Configure trust for the intended issuer,
audience, repository, job

19
00:00:58.185 --> 00:01:01.998
context, and protected environment as
supported by the provider.

20
00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:04.833
Restrict which workflows can request
deployment authority,

21
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:07.766
especially when untrusted pull requests
execute code.

22
00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:12.362
Federation reduces stored keys; it does
not make every build trustworthy.

23
00:01:12.362 --> 00:01:14.904
Test both allowed and denied operations.

24
00:01:14.904 --> 00:01:17.250
Confirm the application starts without
embedded

25
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.183
production credentials and accesses only
intended resources.

26
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:23.312
Verify that unrelated identities cannot
read its secrets

27
00:01:23.312 --> 00:01:26.245
and that cross-environment or cross-tenant
access fails.

28
00:01:26.245 --> 00:01:27.907
Exercise token expiration, credential

29
00:01:27.907 --> 00:01:30.254
refresh, revocation, and dependency
outages.

30
00:01:30.254 --> 00:01:32.600
Check that permissions match actual
application

31
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.240
operations, including background jobs and
migrations.

32
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.249
Provider selection needs an integration
check in a controlled environment:

33
00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:42.866
observe the effective principal in service
audit records, then

34
00:01:42.866 --> 00:01:46.972
remove the intended identity and confirm
failure instead of fallback.

35
00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:49.612
Inspect logs, frontend bundles, source
maps,

36
00:01:49.612 --> 00:01:52.154
and image contents for confidential
material.

37
00:01:52.154 --> 00:01:55.479
Use synthetic test values when checking
leakage controls.

38
00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:59.096
Repository and pipeline secret scanning
adds defense in depth,

39
00:01:59.096 --> 00:02:02.225
but passing a scan does not prove secrets

40
00:02:02.225 --> 00:02:05.940
were never exposed or that identity
permissions are correct.

